It's 4:25 and we are currently writing the script for the video we're shooting tomorrow.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.140
Today we are shooting a video called "Early Access Matrix."

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.300
Why would we figure out the script last second and shoot it the next morning?

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.900
That might seem a little rushed and it-

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.360
and it is. But, why?

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.120
I think we're trying to be consistent. Finally.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.120
Clint and Adrian are working with us in the studio

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.960
We have a lot of filmmakers who are trying to do shorts for the Corridor channel-

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.060
and we're like, okay, you know what?

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.040
Let's- let's up our output. Let's get more consistent. Let's try and find a more regular schedule

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.750
The main point, though, is that we got to be shooting videos more often.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.780
I decided to kick it off by getting this 'Early Access Matrix' video, shot and filmed

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.280
After eight years of filmmaking and doing shorts like this

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.900
it's a little easier for me to roll into something without a plan and then still get a video that turns out.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.500
Brandon once describes the way their schedule works--

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.500
So when we're doing the Freddiew videos,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.800
the way we keep on schedule is

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.820
we picked a date and time that we're going to come out

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.020
So we're weekly- Thursday's at like 9 a.m. For a long time.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.160
We picked that day because we felt like it was open the way the front page at the time worked was like-

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.070
we had slots

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.100
that we wanted to hit to maximize our exposure.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:28.580
We're like, "That's when the next video is gonna be,"

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:34.309
So when it came time to start making it, it's Sunday. We have four days to go through to get that video done,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.920
so we have, you know, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and then up to Thursday.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.840
But we couldn't come up with anything on Sunday,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.760
on Monday the exercise became like this- the pressure mounted, right?

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.240
It's like now it we have to come up with an idea that we need in three days.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.620
that's gonna "wow" the audience and people are going to enjoy it.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.140
Then on Tuesdays, we got two days. Uh, Wednesday, one day. And sometimes we get so far it'll be--

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.440
it'll be Wednesday evening and we still wouldn't have an idea

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.500
but by that point with 12 hours. Like- the pressures really starting to mount

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.500
And interestingly some of the best videos we ever made came out of that system.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.300
Like, for example.... 'Cereal Killer'

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.980
The lessons that we learned doing that was:

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.920
"Try very hard to come up with something earlier and stay ahead of it

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.290
And so put the pressure on that because we know how bad it was snowballed."

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:15.360
But by never letting ourselves slip, it kind of treated through that

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.980
discipline and helped us the exercise of ideation

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.020
Think of coming up with ideas, like a- like a muscle

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:20.840
just like anything else

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.110
It's like "you want a muscle get stronger?"

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.440
You go to the gym, you work out,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.380
you plan activities to make- strengthen that muscle

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.940
If you want to come up with ideas more efficiently,

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.820
you're never going to come up with them unless you're setting a set time

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:30.920
it's like, "We gotta,

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.320
we gotta do it and we got to do it under pressure,"

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.660
and it's- it's interesting is often- like, during those brainstorm sessions,

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.620
we're like, "I need an idea. I need an idea. I need an idea."

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.640
The idea would never- would rarely come during that.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.840
But, then once you're like,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.480
Ugh, let's take a break and go to lunch. *snaps*

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.180
At lunch, that's when you come up the idea.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.320
Or hop in the shower? That's when you come up with the idea.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.120
It would never just happen in a shower

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.160
if you weren't already putting in the hours to, like, try to come up those ideas outside.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.060
It's just interesting but ideas would rarely come during the, like, actual brainstorming;

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:57.600
instead, they come out around it.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.340
Don't fall for us. Our videos are still gonna be pretty big

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.940
We're shooting videos for every single week for at least the next two months.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.320
Everyone knew this video wasn't totally ready,

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.840
but I was still like, "Hey, I'm still shooting. We're still shooting."

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.840
