- Dude!

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.100
All right, so what's a reflector? What is a reflector?

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.440
Anything that's reflective. It's a silk.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:02.500
Well, it's a fine, fine thing made from a- presumably made from like spiders or something.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.680
We go back and forth between using reflectors and silks

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.380
and I kinda wanted to talk about it because we have our own kind of technique that we've developed.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.720
So, our silks and our reflectors are about the same size.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.640
We travel with them. They're supposed to be lightweight,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.520
supposed to be a small crew.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.280
The technique we use to decide whether we're gonna use a reflector or a diffuse

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.780
kind of comes through necessity because we only have reflectors and diffusers of certain sizes.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:23.509
They're not huge.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.540
So when you have sunlight and you're using a reflector

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:27.420
That means you have two sources of light;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.860
and very frequently, if you're using a reflector outside

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.060
you can actually just flatten out your lighting.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.220
Let's say you're backlit by the Sun and then you bounce it back into someone's face,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.980
Well, suddenly you have light everywhere. There's no more shading. There's no more lighting for that matter.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:40.400
Sometimes you kind of need to do that, though.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.429
We can't use the diffuse in the wide so we're gonna use a reflector in the wide

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:44.759
We're gonna even it out by

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.720
shooting the light at an opposite or an oblique angle

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.040
to create a little bit of shading or a little bit of fill and then when we go in close

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.740
We can now use our diffusers to even out the light and get a nice soft closeup on someone's face.

00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:57.199
I personally like that style

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:58.940
It makes it so when you're in a close-up

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.160
you're paying a little more attention to the person's natural features

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.160
rather than the weird lighting

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:06.580
and inconsistent contrast and shading that a

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.380
poorly placed Sun is giving you--

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:09.229
and when you're in a wide

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:14.180
It basically makes it so that way, when you are trying to look at someone's reaction in a wide shot

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.660
there is light hitting it

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.120
and you're able to read it still.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.480
Brings a little clarity to the wides

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.240
and it brings a little beauty the close-ups.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.180
There's no real right or wrong way to use diffuse or reflectors.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.960
You know, when you're filming in Minnesota for example.

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.820
Winter filmmaking in Minnesota,

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.800
your lighting is great all the time

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.640
because the Sun is always just a little bit lower on the horizon.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.620
It's never straight up since you're a little farther north of the Equator

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.560
The sun's always at a lower angle in winter. In those situations,

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.080
I'm definitely using a reflector

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.360
because you have that Sun always kind of at one of these lower angles

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:44.740
You can start playing with your light

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:46.600
When it's top down though, you gotta diffuse it.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.340
You got to diffuse it or else people just- their faces get all messed up.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.200
Inside the battery, there's an accelerometer.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.820
And every time it experiences a shock of more than 100 G--

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.180
and 100 G sounds pretty bad--

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.380
But actually that's only a drop from about two feet onto a hard floor.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:00.980
Every time it experiences a drop like that,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.740
it records it and it actually creates time and date stamp as well

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:07.220
So you can- you can see when your battery was dropped.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:08.100
Put that that on a charger or something

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.660
you can pull that information off

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.100
If the battery is still functional,

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.140
keep using it.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.400
They're gonna experience knocks and bangs during their life

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.020
And one thing you don't want to worry about is your battery failing

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.540
so we build it so it doesn't.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.540
Hey, Carmichael. You wanna see a trick?

